---
/**
 * A RUN-IN HEAD: the opening words of a paragraph that name the point it
 * makes, set on the same line as the paragraph rather than above it.
 *
 *   <RunIn>It has to help us breathe</RunIn>. When being involved…
 *
 * These were italic, which is also how the prose stresses a word, so a new
 * point and an emphasised phrase looked identical. Semibold roman in the
 * working face separates the two jobs: italic is left meaning emphasis only,
 * and a reader skimming a section sees its points line up down the page.
 *
 * Not a heading element. A run-in head belongs to its paragraph, and the
 * document outline should not gain an entry for every point in a list.
 *
 * The full stop goes OUTSIDE the component, in the prose, so it is set in
 * the paragraph's weight rather than the head's.
 */
---

<span class="runin"><slot /></span>

<style>
  .runin {
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* A POINT IS A GROUP OF PARAGRAPHS, not one. The paragraph a run-in head
     opens can be followed by others that continue the same point without a
     head of their own, and at even spacing the bold lines land at irregular
     intervals with nothing to say which paragraphs belong to which. Doubling
     the space ABOVE each head, and only there, groups them by proximity:
     tight within a point, open between points.

     Not after a heading, which already opens with its own space. */
  :global(.prose p:has(> .runin:first-child):not(:is(h2, h3) + p)) {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-7);
  }
</style>
